#{[{:activity/id #uuid "1129f5af-8b20-5759-9f3e-97b087634b1b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134929 :tutorial/content "**Periodic functions** are special functions that repeat themselves every $c$ units. The smallest positive value of $c$ that has this property is called the **period** of the function.\n\nLet's take the following periodic function as an example:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2148/Source/Images/297884a427a047f5b0209ac6933e06b2.png)\n\nThis function repeats itself with a period of $2$. To illustrate, first focus on the part of the graph shown in blue below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2148/Source/Images/f553c854f69e02657b402b40469a7078.png)\n\nIf we shift this part $2$ units to the right, the graph remains unchanged!\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2148/Source/Images/90b02afb5183c653fe9bc0e6bff04426.png)\n\nThe same would happen if we shifted the part $2$ units to the left.\n\nBy knowing the period of a periodic function, we can use a value of the function to infer many other values of the function. For example, in the function above, note that\n\n$$\nf(4)=0\n$$\n\nSince the period is $2$, if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $2$ from the input, the output stays the same. We can write this as\n\n$$\nf(4+2n) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nFor example, setting\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3\\ldots\n$$\n\ngives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn = 1: f(4 + 2(1)) &= f(6) = 0 \\\\\nn = 2: f(4 + 2(2)) &= f(8) = 0 \\\\\nn = 3: f(4 + 2(3)) &= f(10) = 0 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same is true for negative values of $n$. Setting\n\n$$\nn=-1,-2,-3\\ldots\n$$\n\ngives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn =-1: f(4 + 2(-1)) &= f(2) = 0 \\\\\nn =-2: f(4 + 2(-2)) &= f(0) = 0 \\\\\nn =-3: f(4 + 2(-3)) &= f(-2) = 0 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b8020c2e-cfe4-5187-94df-23d7d0fcb605" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1502 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fa1d756d-c380-5339-a443-11ba085cc909" :step/math-academy-id 3664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134802}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133393} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ebe5738-7682-55ad-a21b-235063f08b88" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049518}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134270}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Period of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "5c25e487-4990-59fc-bfc4-1a9c9ad30723" :step/math-academy-id 3665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133394} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57b95b5b-8834-5d1a-8380-fd8889e05cb4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134411}] :knowledge-point/title "Drawing the Graph of a Periodic Function"} :step/id #uuid "e726ab95-f076-53ce-a8d3-da7938f96dec" :step/math-academy-id 3668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133395} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "282b873c-1ba0-5205-b75f-c58721ffaaff" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134412}] :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Values of a Periodic Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "19fd2e5e-dcb6-5935-bb5a-e6645996a5d3" :step/math-academy-id 3671}] :activity/title "Periodic Functions"}]}
